A route counter is written with one branch going the wrong way.
static int routes(int r, int rows) {
  if (r == rows) return 1;
  return routes(r + 1, rows) + routes(r - 1, rows);
}
Call routes(0, 3) with a call counter installed. What does the run show?

A stair helper takes steps of two.
static int climb(int n) {
  if (n == 0) return 0;
  return climb(n - 2);
}
Executed, climb(4) returns normally. What does climb(5) do?

Fill the blank so the helper totals a half-open range without running off the end.
static int total(int[] a, int lo, int hi) {
  if (lo > hi) return 0;
  return a[lo] + total(a, lo /* blank */, hi);
}

Count every invocation of this helper, the first one included.
static final int[] COINS = {1, 2, 5};
static int ways(int amount, int k) {
  if (amount == 0) return 1;
  if (amount < 0 || k == COINS.length) return 0;
  return ways(amount - COINS[k], k) + ways(amount, k + 1);
}
How many invocations does ways(7, 0) make?

A developer adds a cache but keys it on the amount alone.
static int[] memo = new int[12];
static int ways(int amount, int k) {
  if (amount == 0) return 1;
  if (amount < 0 || k == COINS.length) return 0;
  if (memo[amount] != -1) return memo[amount];
  return memo[amount] = ways(amount - COINS[k], k) + ways(amount, k + 1);
}
With COINS = {1, 2, 5}, what does ways(7, 0) return?

Add the cache and count only the frames that actually compute a new pair.
static int ways(int amount, int k) {
  if (amount == 0) return 1;
  if (amount < 0 || k == COINS.length) return 0;
  if (memo[amount][k] != -1) return memo[amount][k];
  memo[amount][k] = ways(amount - COINS[k], k) + ways(amount, k + 1);
  return memo[amount][k];
}
With COINS = {1, 2, 5} and a fresh table, how many pairs does ways(7, 0) compute?

Fill both blanks so the helper reuses a computed pair instead of solving it twice.
static int ways(int amount, int k) {
  if (amount == 0) return 1;
  if (amount < 0 || k == COINS.length) return 0;
  if (memo[amount][k] != -1) return /* blank */;
  /* blank */ = ways(amount - COINS[k], k) + ways(amount, k + 1);
  return memo[amount][k];
}

A developer tries 0 as the not-yet-computed marker.
static int[] memo = new int[12];
static int ways(int amount, int k) {
  if (amount == 0) return 1;
  if (amount < 0 || k == COINS.length) return 0;
  if (memo[amount] == 0) return memo[amount];
  return memo[amount] = ways(amount - COINS[k], k) + ways(amount, k + 1);
}
With COINS = {1, 2, 5}, what does ways(7, 0) return?

A factored helper returns all subsets of a 4-item set.
static List<String> subs(char[] items, int i) {
  if (i == items.length) return new ArrayList<>(List.of(""));
  List<String> tail = subs(items, i + 1);
  List<String> out = new ArrayList<>(tail.size() * 2);
  for (String s : tail) out.add(items[i] + s);
  for (String s : tail) out.add(s);
  return out;
}
Run subs on {a, b, c, d} and count the items actually stored. How many are there?

A subset helper writes into the caller's list and hands the same list back.
static void subs(char[] items, int i, List<String> out) {
  if (i == items.length) { out.add(""); return; }
  int mark = out.size();
  subs(items, i + 1, out);
  List<String> tail = new ArrayList<>(out.subList(mark, out.size()));
  for (String s : tail) out.add(items[i] + s);
  for (String s : tail) out.add(s);
}
Call subs(new char[]{'a','b','c','d'}, 0, new ArrayList<>()). How many entries does out hold?

Count every invocation of this enumerator, the first one included.
static void subsets(char[] items, int i, StringBuilder cur, List<String> out) {
  if (i == items.length) { out.add(cur.toString()); return; }
  cur.append(items[i]);
  subsets(items, i + 1, cur, out);
  cur.setLength(cur.length() - 1);
  subsets(items, i + 1, cur, out);
}
How many invocations occur for a 4-item set?

Fill the blanks so both branches of the grid walk consult the one table.
static int cell(int r, int c, int[][] memo) {
  if (r == ROWS - 1 || c == COLS - 1) return 1;
  if (memo[r][c] != -1) return memo[r][c];
  return memo[r][c] = cell(r + 1, c, /* blank */)
      + cell(r, c + 1, /* blank */);
}

A recursive join returns a[i] followed by the rest, and a developer rewrites it as a loop.
static String rec(String[] a, int i) {
  if (i == a.length) return "";
  return a[i] + rec(a, i + 1);
}
static String loopWrong(String[] a) {
  StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
  for (int i = a.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) sb.append(a[i]);
  return sb.toString();
}
For {"a", "b", "c"}, how do the two results differ?

One builder is shared by every frame of this method.
static void build(StringBuilder sb, int n) {
  if (n == 0) return;
  sb.append(n).append(',');
  build(sb, n - 1);
  sb.append(n).append(',');
}
Run build(new StringBuilder(), 3). What does the builder hold afterwards?

A search over subsets adds each item, recurses, and prints when it runs out of items.
static void show(List<String> chosen, String[] items, int i) {
  if (i == items.length) { System.out.println(chosen); return; }
  chosen.add(items[i]);
  show(chosen, items, i + 1);
  show(chosen, items, i + 1);
}
Run show on {"a", "b", "c"}. What is printed?

Fill the blank so every printed subset is correct for {a, b, c}.
static void show(List<String> chosen, String[] items, int i) {
  if (i == items.length) { System.out.println(chosen); return; }
  chosen.add(items[i]);
  show(chosen, items, i + 1);
  /* blank */
  show(chosen, items, i + 1);
}

One array is passed to four separate calls, each with a different start index.
static void fill(int[] a, int i) {
  if (i == a.length) return;
  a[i] = i * i;
  fill(a, i + 1);
}
static int[] run() {
  int[] row = new int[4];
  for (int r = 0; r < 4; r++) fill(row, r);
  return row;
}
What does run() return?

A reporter claims to list nodes level by level but writes this.
static void report(Node n, List<String> out) {
  out.add(n.name);
  for (Node k : n.kids) report(k, out);
}
For a root with children B and C, where B has children D and E, what order comes out?

The tree is A with children B and C; B has D and E; C has F and G.
static void walk(Node n, List<String> out) {
  out.add(n.name);
  for (Node k : n.kids) walk(k, out);
}
Run walk on A. What order is appended?

A level-order walk enqueues the root's children twice.
ArrayDeque<Node> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
q.add(root);
boolean first = true;
while (!q.isEmpty()) {
  Node n = q.remove();
  System.out.print(n.name + " ");
  if (first) { q.addAll(n.kids); first = false; }
  q.addAll(n.kids);
}
For the same seven-node tree, what is printed?

A helper is meant to report whether every reading is positive.
static boolean allPositive(int[] a, int i, boolean ok) {
  if (i == a.length) return ok;
  boolean next = a[i] > 0;
  return allPositive(a, i + 1, next);
}
Run allPositive on {1, 2, -5, 4} starting with ok = true. What comes back?

Instrument every invocation of this predicate, the first one included.
static int firstBad(int[] a, int i) {
  if (i == a.length) return 0;
  if (a[i] < 0) return i;
  return firstBad(a, i + 1);
}
Run firstBad on {5, 7, -1, 9, -2} starting at index 0. How many invocations occur?

Two helpers are written to test even and odd numbers.
static boolean isEven(int n) { return n == 0 ? true : isOdd(n - 1); }
static boolean isOdd(int n) { return isEven(n); }
Executed, what does isEven(4) do?
